1
00:00:08,736 --> 00:00:14,626
The heritage corners tell science through people, places and works.

2
00:00:14,686 --> 00:00:22,063
Every corner has short, sourced cards, two activities and related games.

3
00:00:22,773 --> 00:00:31,663
From Baku to Samarkand, from Bishkek to Lefkoşa: a shared language family and a great scientific heritage.

4
00:00:31,723 --> 00:00:38,334
Al-Khwarizmi, al-Biruni and Ulugh Beg are part of this heritage.

5
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:50,244
A museum in Nuremberg that has welcomed tens of thousands of visitors a year since 1995: coins, clothes, carpets, calligraphy and tiles.

6
00:00:50,304 --> 00:00:56,639
Its founder, Cemalettin Özdemir, guides a five-stop tour.

7
00:00:57,349 --> 00:01:04,319
A thousand years ago al-Biruni measured the size of the Earth from a mountaintop.

8
00:01:04,379 --> 00:01:10,876
The science bridges tell the science stories that link two countries.

9
00:01:11,586 --> 00:01:18,476
Japan and Türkiye work together to understand earthquakes and reduce their harm.

10
00:01:18,536 --> 00:01:27,148
Japanese archaeologists have worked at Kaman-Kalehöyük in Kırşehir since 1985.

11
00:01:27,858 --> 00:01:34,368
János Bolyai and İbrahim Müteferrika were born in the same city, Kolozsvár.

12
00:01:34,428 --> 00:01:41,385
Bolyai built a new geometry; Ernő Rubik made his cube in 1974.

13
00:01:42,095 --> 00:01:54,985
In the Republic’s first years the language, measures and calendar of science were rebuilt: a new alphabet, the metre and kilogram, the international calendar.

14
00:01:55,045 --> 00:01:59,690
Atatürk also wrote a geometry book.

15
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:09,060
The work of Aziz Sancar, Cahit Arf, Feza Gürsey, Oktay Sinanoğlu, Canan Dağdeviren and three academics.

16
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:15,961
The cards carry no photos; ideas and works come first.

17
00:02:16,671 --> 00:02:27,641
Fuat Sezgin researched the history of science in the Islamic world and gathered its works, from medicine to astronomy, in 17 volumes.

18
00:02:27,701 --> 00:02:34,266
He founded an institute and a museum in Frankfurt.

19
00:02:34,976 --> 00:02:43,866
Hundreds of buildings are attributed to Mimar Sinan; he considered the Selimiye in Edirne his finest work.

20
00:02:43,926 --> 00:02:50,537
The corner explains arches, domes and protection against earthquakes.

21
00:02:51,247 --> 00:03:00,837
Eight science bridges tell the science stories that link two countries, with short cards, two activities and games.

22
00:03:00,897 --> 00:03:08,843
They focus on science and shared heritage, not on current politics.

23
00:03:09,553 --> 00:03:15,523
Each card shows its sources, and the activities make students think.

24
00:03:15,583 --> 00:03:27,148
Corners lead on to related games: Ulugh Beg’s Observatory, al-Khwarizmi’s Balance, al-Biruni Measures the Earth.

